\begin_layout Section
Operators and Spheres
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Optical elements vs.
 quantum gates:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Jones matrices (2 × 2 complex matrices) act on Jones vectors just as unitary operators or Pauli matrices act on two-level kets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Poincaré sphere vs.
 Bloch sphere:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Both map pure states onto a unit sphere where orthogonal states sit at opposite poles.
 Note the angle-doubling factor:
 a 90° physical polarization rotation or a 180° spin-1/2 state flip moves 180° around the sphere.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Key Physical Differences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Classical wave vs.
 quantum state:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 A Jones vector describes classical transverse electromagnetic oscillations (or a single photon's polarization degree of freedom) whereas a spin ket describes a quantum state whose squared amplitudes give measurement probabilities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
• Opposite physical directions on the sphere:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 For spin-1/2 opposite poles of the Bloch sphere correspond to opposite physical directions in 3D space (e.g.,
 +z and -z).
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 For Jones vectors orthogonal linear polarizations (x and y) are 90° apart in the transverse plane not 180° opposite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Mixed / unpolarized light:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 A single Jones vector only represents fully polarized light (like a pure ket).
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Unpolarized or partially polarized light requires a 2 × 2 coherency/density matrix rather than a single ket.
\end_layout

