\begin_layout Subsection
Why Photons Only Have Two States Despite Being Spin-1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Normally a spin-1 particle has three spin projections (
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{-1,0,+1}$
\end_inset

) along any axis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Because a photon is massless and travels at the speed of light,
 relativistic invariance forbids the 
\backslash
(0
\backslash
) projection along its direction of motion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This leaves only two helicity states (
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{+1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 corresponding to right and left circular polarization) allowing any polarization to be written in a 2D Hilbert space just like a spin-1/2 system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Geometry:
 90° vs.
 180°
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Electrons:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Spin-1/2 states point directly along 3D spatial axes.
 Spin-up along 
\begin_inset Formula $+z$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $\vert\uparrow\rangle$
\end_inset

) and spin-down along 
\begin_inset Formula $-z$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $\vert\downarrow\rangle$
\end_inset

) are physically opposite in space so rotating an electron by 180° turns 
\begin_inset Formula $\vert{}\uparrow\rangle$
\end_inset

 into 
\begin_inset Formula $\vert\downarrow\rangle$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Photons:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Linear polarization is restricted to the 2D plane perpendicular to the beam.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Horizontal (
\begin_inset Formula $\vert H\rangle$
\end_inset

) and vertical (
\begin_inset Formula $\vert V\rangle$
\end_inset

) polarizations are orthogonal quantum states even though their electric fields are only 90° apart physically.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Behavior Under a 360° Rotation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Photons:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Rotating a polarizer or photon state by 360° leaves the state completely unchanged (
\begin_inset Formula $\vert\psi\rangle\to+\vert\psi\rangle$
\end_inset

,
 which is the hallmark of an integer-spin boson.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Electrons:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Rotating a spin-1/2 particle by 360° multiplies its quantum state by 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{-1}(\ensuremath{\vert\psi\rangle\to-\vert\psi\rangle}$
\end_inset

).
 It takes a 720° rotation to bring the spinor back to its original sign,
 ifference observable in neutron and electron interferometry.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Walk through of a Pauli matrix vs.
 Jones matrix example
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A half-wave plate (HWP) rotated at 45° has the same matrix representation as the Pauli-
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 matrix swapping horizontal and vertical polarization just as 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{x}$
\end_inset

 flips spin-up and spin-down.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
1The Matrix Setup
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In both systems,
 we use the standard two-element basis:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Optics basis:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vert{}H\rangle=\begin{pmatrix}1\\
0
\end{pmatrix}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vert V\rangle=\begin{pmatrix}0\\
1
\end{pmatrix}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Quantum spin basis:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vert0\rangle=\begin{pmatrix}1\\
0
\end{pmatrix}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vert1\rangle=\begin{pmatrix}0\\
1
\end{pmatrix}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Pauli-
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 matrix is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{x}=\left(\begin{matrix}0 & 1\\
1 & 0
\end{matrix}\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a half-wave plate with its fast axis tilted at an angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\theta}$
\end_inset

 from horizontal the general Jones matrix (up to a global phase) is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
J_{\text{HWP}}(\theta) & = & \left(\begin{matrix}\cos2\theta & \sin2\theta\\
\sin2\theta & -\cos2\theta
\end{matrix}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Setting 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=45^{\circ}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Plugging 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=45^{\circ}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $2\theta=90^{\circ}$
\end_inset

) into the Jones matrix gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\cos90^{\circ}=0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sin90^{\circ}=1$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
J_{\text{HWP}}(45^{\circ}) & = & \left(\begin{matrix}0 & 1\\
1 & 0
\end{matrix}\right)\\
 & = & \sigma_{x}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Step-by-Step Action on a State
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Start with horizontally polarized light,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vert{}H\rangle=\begin{pmatrix}1\\
0
\end{pmatrix}$
\end_inset

,
 and send it through the 45°-tilted half-wave plate:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
J_{\text{HWP}}(45^{\circ})|H\rangle & = & \left(\begin{matrix}0 & 1\\
1 & 0
\end{matrix}\right)\left(\begin{matrix}1\\
0
\end{matrix}\right)\\
 & = & \left(\begin{matrix}0\cdot1+1\cdot0\\
1\cdot1+0\cdot0
\end{matrix}\right)\\
 & = & \left(\begin{matrix}0\\
1
\end{matrix}\right)\\
 & = & |V\rangle
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In optics:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The plate introduces a 
\backslash
(
\backslash
pi 
\backslash
) (half-wavelength) phase shift between the fast and slow axes at
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\pm45^{\circ}\$}$
\end_inset

) rotating the polarization vector by
\begin_inset Formula $2\theta=90^{\circ}$
\end_inset

 from horizontal to vertical.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In quantum mechanics:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Applying 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{x}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\vert0\rangle$
\end_inset

 flips the ket to 
\begin_inset Formula $\vert1\rangle$
\end_inset

 thereby rotating the state vector by 
\begin_inset Formula $180^{\circ}$
\end_inset

 around the 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

-axis of the Bloch sphere.
\end_layout

